// Practice quiz: Dependency injection replacing manual wiring
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/spring-boot-basics/dependency-injection-replacing-manual-wiring

::o1 who wires::After marking a controller, service and repository as components with constructor parameters, who calls their constructors? {
~Your main method#Not quite. Your main method only starts the application.
=The Spring container#Correct. It creates beans in dependency order and supplies the parameters.
~The JVM class loader#Not quite. Class loading does not create these objects.
~The Maven build#Not quite. Maven builds the code; it does not run it.
}

::o1 final field::Why keep an injected dependency in a final field? {
=It must be set during construction and cannot change later#Correct. The object is never half-built and the dependency stays fixed.
~Spring requires final on all fields#Not quite. Spring does not require this; it is a design choice.
~Final fields run faster at runtime#Not quite. Speed is not the reason.
~It makes the bean a prototype#Not quite. Scope is unrelated to final.
}

::o2 missing bean::A constructor needs a type with no bean in the container. What happens? {
~The field stays null until first use#Not quite. Required constructor dependencies are not left null.
~Spring creates a default instance with new#Not quite. It does not guess implementations.
~The first request returns 404#Not quite. The failure happens earlier, at startup.
=Startup fails naming the missing type#Correct. Spring refuses to start rather than inject null.
}

::o2 ambiguity::Two beans match one injection point and neither is marked specially. What is a valid fix for one specific consumer? {
=Use @Qualifier at that injection point#Correct. A qualifier names the wanted bean right where it is needed.
~Add @Autowired to the constructor#Not quite. That does not resolve which bean matches.
~Declare the field static#Not quite. Static does not affect bean selection.
~Reorder the classes in the file#Not quite. Spring does not pick by source order.
}

::o3 testability::What does constructor injection let a plain unit test do? {
~Skip writing any fake#Not quite. You still need to supply a dependency.
~Avoid assertions#Not quite. Injection style has no effect on assertions.
=Create the class with new and pass a fake#Correct. No Spring context is needed.
~Run only inside the full application#Not quite. That is exactly what it avoids.
}

